Let \(S\subseteq R_0\subseteq\mathbb{R}^n\), and let \(T_{
u}\) denote the basic-step convexification operator associated with an admissible selected combination \(
u\). The formulation is sequentially convexifiable from \(R_0\) if there exists a finite sequence \((
u_1,\ldots,
u_m)\) such that \[R_k:=T_{
u_k}(R_{k-1})\quad(k=1,\ldots,m),\qquad R_m=\operatorname{cl}\operatorname{conv}(S).\] Exactness is required only at the final stage; intermediate relaxations may strictly contain the target hull.

Given a basic-step sequence \((T_{
u_1},\ldots,T_{
u_m})\) with relaxations \(R_k=T_{
u_k}(R_{k-1})\), call the sequence face-compatible when, at every stage \(k\), each nonempty term \(R_{k-1}\cap P_{k,i}\) of the selected disjunction is a face of \(R_{k-1}\), and every such face containing a target point intersects \(S\). Formally, \[R_{k-1}\cap P_{k,i}\neq\varnothing\Longrightarrow R_{k-1}\cap P_{k,i}\unlhd R_{k-1},\] together with \[(R_{k-1}\cap P_{k,i})\cap S\neq\varnothing\] whenever the term is retained.

